  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,\u27f9\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
